A. abstract class defines what an object is (identity and shared state), whereas an interface defines what an object can do (a behavioral contract)

Feature	
Abstract Class	Interface
Inheritance	
Single inheritance only (a class can inherit from only one class).	
Multiple implementation (a class can implement multiple interfaces).

Instance Fields	
Can contain state/instance fields (variables).	
Cannot contain instance fields.

Constructors	
Can have constructors to initialize base state.	
Cannot have constructors.

Access Modifiers	
Can use any modifier (public, protected, private, etc.).	
Historically strictly public. Modern C# allows modifiers for default implementations.

Default Logic	
Can provide fully implemented concrete methods.	
Can contain Default Interface Methods (introduced in C# 8.0).

Performance	
Slightly faster direct method invocation.	
Marginally slower due to interface lookup tables

