The N+1 query problem is a common database performance anti-pattern where an application executes one initial query to retrieve a list of records, 
followed by N subsequent queries to fetch related data for each item in that list.

Instead of fetching all required data in a single efficient operation, the application repeatedly communicates with the database inside a loop, causing significant latency and performance bottlenecks.

An Example of the Problem

Imagine you want to display a list of 100 blog posts along with the name of each post's author.
1. The "1" Query: Your Object-Relational Mapper (ORM) or code fetches the posts
SELECT * FROM posts;

2. The "N" Queries: Your code loops through the 100 posts to display the author's name. Because the author data wasn't loaded initially, the ORM triggers a separate query for every single post:
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1;
SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 2;
-- ... this repeats 100 times!

Total Queries Executed: 1 + 100 = 101 queries to display a simple list. 
If you have 10,000 records, it will fire 10,001 queries, crashing performance in production.

🛑 Why Does It Happen?
The problem most frequently occurs when using Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) tools (like Hibernate, Entity Framework, Django ORM, or ActiveRecord) that utilize Lazy Loading.
• Lazy loading delays loading related data until the application explicitly asks for it (e.g., calling post.author.name).
• When this property access happens inside a loop iterating over the parent records, it silently hammers the database with dozens or hundreds of individual requests.


🛠️ How to Fix It
The standard solution is to instruct your application to load the parent and child records at the same time, a concept known as Eager Loading.

Strategy | How it Works	 | Example Framework Methods
SQL JOIN (Fetch Joins) | Combines the parent and child tables into a single query using a LEFT JOIN. |	JPA/Hibernate: JOIN FETCH
Pre-fetching / Batching	 | Executes exactly 2 queries: One to get all parent rows, and a second bulk query using an IN clause to get all matching child rows. | EF Core: .Include()

By switching to these methods, you reduce your database footprint from N+1 queries down to just 1 or 2 queries, instantly restoring application speed.

Use the .Include() extension method to build your relation execution plan. 
If child collections are massive, chain .AsSplitQuery() to prevent a performance-degrading Cartesian product explosion
// EF Core syntax
var authors = await _context.Authors
    .Include(a => a.Books)
    .ToListAsync();
