In .NET Core (and .NET 8+) running on Azure, logging is implemented via the built-in ILogger abstraction and seamlessly integrated with cloud-native monitoring through Azure Monitor Application Insights.


1. Architectural Blueprint
Instead of writing logs to local files, the best practice is to pipe logs to the standard console output in JSON format or stream them directly via an SDK.


2. Code Implementation
To integrate your .NET application with Azure, you rely on the official Application Insights Telemetry SDK.

Step 1: Install NuGet Packages
Add the tracking and logging extensions to your microservice project:
• Microsoft.ApplicationInsights.AspNetCore
• Serilog.Extensions.Logging & Serilog.Sinks.ApplicationInsights (Optional, if using Serilog for custom JSON formatting).

Step 2: Register Services (Program.cs)
Register Application Insights in your service collection. It automatically hooks into the native .NET ILogger pipeline.

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
// Add Application Insights Telemetry
// This automatically captures requests, dependencies, and exceptions
builder.Services.AddApplicationInsightsTelemetry(options =>
{
    // The connection string is read implicitly from configuration (APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING)
});

var app = builder.Build();


Step 3: Inject and Use ILogger
Inject the standard ILogger<T> into your controllers or services. Structured logging uses placeholder tokens to preserve parameters.

[ApiController]
[Route("[controller]")]
public class OrderController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly ILogger<OrderController> _logger;

    public OrderController(ILogger<OrderController> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult CreateOrder([FromBody] OrderRequest request)
    {
        // Structured logging: Azure extracts OrderId and CustomerId as searchable metadata fields
        _logger.LogInformation("Processing order {OrderId} for customer {CustomerId}.", request.OrderId, request.CustomerId);

        try
        {
            // Business logic here
            return Ok();
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // Pass the exception object as the first parameter so the full stack trace is indexed
            _logger.LogError(ex, "Failed to process order {OrderId}.", request.OrderId);
            return StatusCode(500);
        }
    }
}



3. Configuration (appsettings.json)
Control your log verbosity per environment. For Azure production, you typically want to filter out framework-level Information logs to reduce data ingestion costs
{
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      "Default": "Information",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning",
      "Microsoft.Hosting.Lifetime": "Information"
    },
    "ApplicationInsights": {
      "LogLevel": {
        "Default": "Information",
        "Microsoft": "Warning"
      }
    }
  },
  "APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING": "InstrumentationKey=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000;IngestionEndpoint=https://..."
}


4. Distributed Tracing in Azure
When microservices call each other via HTTP (HttpClient) or messaging queues (like Azure Service Bus), .NET and Application Insights handle request correlation out of the box.
• W3C Trace Context: .NET automatically injects standard headers (traceparent, tracestate) into HTTP requests.
• End-to-End Transaction Map: When downstream services receive these headers, they attach their logs to the same Operation ID. 
In the Azure Portal, you can click on an error or a trace and view the exact hierarchical tree of every microservice call involved in that timeline.


5. Querying Logs in Azure Portal
Once logs are captured, you query them inside your Log Analytics Workspace using Kusto Query Language (KQL).
• Search for custom text or properties:
traces
| where timestamp > ago(1h)
| where severityLevel == 2 // 2 = Information
| extend orderId = customDimensions.Prop__OrderId
| where orderId == "ORD-12345"

Find exceptions linked to specific requests:
exceptions
| where timestamp > ago(24h)
| order by timestamp desc
| project timestamp, operation_Id, outerMessage, innermostMessage
